\end_inset
; the scripts are in research/fem of the repository, and every number below is regenerated by them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Geometry, material and boundary conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A tongue is described in the reed bank as a series of sections from the rivet to the tip, each with its length, its width and its thickness at both ends, and its material; a tip mass can be added. The reference tongue of this chapter is the one Ewen measured in 2020 for the reedgui tool (matrix.txt): four sections over a free length of 37.4 mm, widths from 4.26 to 3.47 mm, thicknesses of 0.35, 0.20 and 0.275 mm, and a 1.5 mm thick tip block over the last 9 mm. Spring steel is taken with 
\begin_inset Formula $E=210$
\end_inset
 GPa, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=7800$
\end_inset
 kg/m
\begin_inset Formula $^{3}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu=0.29$
\end_inset
; handbook values vary by about 3 per cent on 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset
 with the grade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two clampings are compared. The ideal one fixes the whole cross-section at the root of the slot. The realistic one, called rivet clamping, extends the heel 5 mm behind the slot, lets it rest on the plate (no vertical displacement under the heel) and holds it at every point over a 2 mm band under the head of the rivet, 3 mm behind the slot. The realistic clamping lowers 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1}$
\end_inset
 by 0.3 per cent: small, but larger than the remaining discretisation error, so it is the default. The eigenproblem is linear elasticity, solved with the direct solver Umfpack (MUMPS is not shipped with Elmer 26.2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Elements and mesh convergence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tongue is meshed with a structured grid of 20-node quadratic hexahedra: 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{x}$
\end_inset
