 compares the three methods. Elmer and the exact beam agree within 0.03 to 1.1 per cent on five modes, and their first mode shapes differ by 0.09 per cent. The two-mode Ritz model is 51 per cent too high. Its integrals are right; its basis is poor. Global shape functions of a uniform cantilever cannot bend sharply in the thin 0.20 mm hinge just before the 1.5 mm tip block, so the hinge is made artificially stiff. Ritz is an upper bound and converges slowly here (6 per cent with five functions, 1.3 per cent with twenty). The error lies in the stiffness, not in the mass: reduced to the tip, the modal stiffness of the two-mode model is 2.4 times too large while its modal mass is only 4.7 per cent too large. For a uniform blade the same two functions are exact, which is why the problem went unnoticed.
